  <div class="wrap">
<div class="hub">
  <div class="hub-box">
    <div class="hub-title">ASBJ Statement No. 21 — Accounting Standard for Business Combinations</div>
    <div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Japan GAAP (J-GAAP) · 企業結合に関する会計基準 · MAJOR divergence: goodwill is always amortised, unlike IFRS 3/ASC 805 · JPY examples</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="toc"><a href="#s1">1. Conceptual Framework Linkage</a><a href="#s2">2. Scope</a><a href="#s3">3. The Acquisition Method — Identifying the Acquirer</a><a href="#s4">4. Measuring Consideration and Identifiable Net Assets at Fair Value</a><a href="#s5">5. THE KEY DIVERGENCE — Goodwill Is Always Amortised</a><a href="#s6">6. Negative Goodwill (Bargain Purchase Gain)</a><a href="#s7">7. Non-Controlling Interests — the Partial Goodwill Approach Only</a><a href="#s8">8. The Narrowing of Pooling-of-Interests-Style Accounting</a><a href="#s9">9. Worked Example — Goodwill Recognition and Amortisation</a><a href="#s10">10. Worked Example — a Bargain Purchase Gain</a><a href="#s11">11. Disclosure Requirements</a><a href="#s12">12. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</a><a href="#s13">13. J-GAAP vs IFRS 3 — Comprehensive Comparison</a><a href="#s14">14. Statement Mapping</a><a href="#s15">15. Related Standards</a><a href="#s16">16. Glossary</a></div>
<div class="section-header" id="s1">
  <span class="num">1</span>
  <h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Fair-value acquisition accounting, with one of J-GAAP's most significant divergences</div>
    <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 21 requires the ACQUISITION METHOD (fair value-based purchase accounting) for VIRTUALLY ALL business combinations — identifying an ACQUIRER, measuring consideration TRANSFERRED at fair value, and recognising the acquiree's IDENTIFIABLE assets and liabilities at fair value, with GOODWILL recognised for any excess. It is the NATURAL companion to ASBJ Statement No. 7 (Business Divestitures), addressing the OTHER side of the SAME transactions. It is ALSO home to one of the MOST significant, well-known divergences from IFRS and US GAAP in the ENTIRE J-GAAP suite: the MANDATORY AMORTISATION of goodwill (Section 5).</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s2">
  <span class="num">2</span>
  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Business combinations where one entity obtains control of another</div>
    <div class="principle">This Statement applies to BUSINESS COMBINATIONS — transactions or events in which an ACQUIRER obtains CONTROL of one or more businesses — whether structured through share acquisition, asset acquisition, or a Companies Act corporate reorganisation mechanism (see the companion ASBJ Statement No. 7 poster for the corporate split backdrop).</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s3">
  <span class="num">3</span>
  <h2>The Acquisition Method — Identifying the Acquirer</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Every combination has an identifiable acquirer</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">The ACQUISITION METHOD requires IDENTIFYING which combining entity is the "ACQUIRER" — generally the entity obtaining CONTROL — based on indicators such as RELATIVE size, WHO issues consideration, and WHO dominates management of the combined entity AFTERWARD. Once identified, the acquirer applies purchase accounting FROM the acquirer's own perspective.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s4">
  <span class="num">4</span>
  <h2>Measuring Consideration and Identifiable Net Assets at Fair Value</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">Fair value throughout — consideration, and acquired assets/liabilities</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Consideration TRANSFERRED (cash, shares issued, contingent consideration) is measured at FAIR VALUE at the acquisition date. The acquiree's IDENTIFIABLE assets acquired and liabilities assumed are ALSO recognised at their INDIVIDUAL fair values — REGARDLESS of their PREVIOUS carrying amounts in the acquiree's own books — with GOODWILL recognised for any EXCESS of consideration over the fair value of net identifiable assets acquired.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s5">
  <span class="num">5</span>
  <h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE — Goodwill Is Always Amortised</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">A systematic charge over a period not exceeding 20 years — no indefinite-life exception</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">UNLIKE IFRS 3 (and US GAAP's ASC 350, post-2001), which treat GOODWILL as an INDEFINITE-life asset TESTED ONLY for impairment (never systematically amortised), J-GAAP requires goodwill to be<strong>SYSTEMATICALLY AMORTISED</strong>— generally on a STRAIGHT-LINE basis — over a period the entity determines is APPROPRIATE, but NOT EXCEEDING 20 YEARS, in ADDITION to being tested for IMPAIRMENT where indicators exist. This is one of the MOST SIGNIFICANT, FREQUENTLY-CITED divergences between J-GAAP and international practice — it means Japanese companies' reported profit is SYSTEMATICALLY REDUCED by goodwill amortisation EVERY period following an acquisition, in a way that companies reporting under IFRS or US GAAP simply do NOT experience (their profit is affected ONLY if and when an impairment is triggered).</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s6">
  <span class="num">6</span>
  <h2>Negative Goodwill (Bargain Purchase Gain)</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">Recognised immediately in profit or loss — converged with IFRS 3</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Where the fair value of NET IDENTIFIABLE assets acquired EXCEEDS the consideration transferred (a "BARGAIN PURCHASE"), the RESULTING gain is recognised IMMEDIATELY in the Statement of Income — CONSISTENT with IFRS 3's OWN treatment of negative goodwill, a genuinely CONVERGED area despite goodwill's OTHERWISE divergent treatment.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s7">
  <span class="num">7</span>
  <h2>Non-Controlling Interests — the Partial Goodwill Approach Only</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">No "full goodwill" election available, unlike IFRS 3</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Non-controlling interests are measured at their PROPORTIONATE SHARE of the acquiree's IDENTIFIABLE net assets (the "PARTIAL GOODWILL" method) — meaning goodwill is recognised ONLY in respect of the PARENT's OWN acquired interest, NOT the non-controlling interest's share. This is J-GAAP's ONLY available approach — UNLIKE IFRS 3, which OFFERS a transaction-by-transaction ELECTION between the partial goodwill method and the "FULL GOODWILL" method (measuring non-controlling interests at FAIR VALUE, recognising goodwill attributable to the ENTIRE acquiree, including the non-controlling portion).</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s8">
  <span class="num">8</span>
  <h2>The Narrowing of Pooling-of-Interests-Style Accounting</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">A convergence-driven elimination, mirroring IFRS 3 and ASC 805</div>
    <div class="text-supporting">Japan historically permitted a "UNITING OF INTERESTS" (pooling-of-interests-style) method for combinations meeting NARROW criteria (no identifiable acquirer, broadly EQUAL relative size, consideration entirely in voting shares) — but this eligibility has been SIGNIFICANTLY NARROWED over successive revisions, moving J-GAAP TOWARD requiring acquisition accounting for VIRTUALLY ALL business combinations, MIRRORING how BOTH IFRS 3 and US GAAP's ASC 805 ALSO eliminated pooling-of-interests accounting internationally around a broadly SIMILAR period.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s9">
  <span class="num">9</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — Goodwill Recognition and Amortisation</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cw span2">
    <div class="ctitle cw">A systematic annual charge, unlike an impairment-only approach</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: An acquisition generating ¥600,000,000 of goodwill</div>
      <div class="wk-body">A company acquires 100% of a target for ¥2,000,000,000 cash. The fair value of the target's identifiable net assets is ¥1,400,000,000. The acquirer determines a 15-year amortisation period is appropriate.</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">GOODWILL = ¥2,000,000,000 − ¥1,400,000,000 =<strong>¥600,000,000</strong>. This is AMORTISED on a straight-line basis: ¥600,000,000 ÷ 15 =<strong>¥40,000,000 PER YEAR</strong>, recognised as an expense EVERY year for 15 years — REGARDLESS of whether any impairment indicator exists — a MATERIAL, RECURRING charge that would NOT arise under IFRS 3 or ASC 805, where the SAME ¥600,000,000 goodwill would simply be tested ANNUALLY for impairment.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s10">
  <span class="num">10</span>
  <h2>Worked Example — a Bargain Purchase Gain</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Immediate recognition, converged with IFRS 3</div>
    <div class="worked worked--flush">
      <div class="wk-title">Example: Acquiring a distressed target below fair value</div>
      <div class="wk-body">A company acquires 100% of a distressed target for ¥500,000,000 cash. The fair value of the target's identifiable net assets is ¥620,000,000.</div>
      <div class="wk-answer">The company recognises a BARGAIN PURCHASE GAIN of ¥620,000,000 − ¥500,000,000 =<strong>¥120,000,000</strong>, IMMEDIATELY in the Statement of Income — a treatment CONSISTENT with IFRS 3's own approach to negative goodwill.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s11">
  <span class="num">11</span>
  <h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 21 requires</div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>Nature and terms of the business combination, consideration transferred, and fair values of identifiable assets/liabilities acquired</span><span>Amount of goodwill (or bargain purchase gain) recognised, and the amortisation period and method for goodwill</span><span>Pro forma revenue/profit information where practicable</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s12">
  <span class="num">12</span>
  <h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying the acquirer where it is not immediately obvious</span><span>Determining an appropriate goodwill amortisation period, up to the 20-year maximum</span></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
        <div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of consideration transferred and identifiable assets/liabilities acquired</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s13">
  <span class="num">13</span>
  <h2>J-GAAP vs IFRS 3 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card ci span2">
    <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IFRS 3</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 21)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 3)</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Goodwill subsequent treatment</span><span>SYSTEMATICALLY AMORTISED, max 20 years, plus impairment testing</span><span>NOT amortised — impairment testing ONLY</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Non-controlling interests measurement</span><span>Partial goodwill method ONLY</span><span>ELECTION between partial and full goodwill methods</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Bargain purchase gains</span><span>Immediate recognition in profit or loss</span><span>Immediate recognition in profit or loss — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Pooling-of-interests</span><span>Narrowly eliminated over successive revisions</span><span>Eliminated — converged in overall direction</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">The GOODWILL AMORTISATION requirement is widely regarded as ONE of the SINGLE most significant remaining divergences between J-GAAP and IFRS/US GAAP — a genuinely MATERIAL, RECURRING difference in reported profitability for any acquisitive Japanese company.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s14">
  <span class="num">14</span>
  <h2>Statement Mapping</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cs span2">
    <div class="ctitle cs">Where every business combination event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Initial goodwill recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Recognised as the excess of consideration over fair value of net identifiable assets</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Goodwill amortisation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Recognised every period, up to 20 years</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Bargain purchase gain</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Recognised immediately</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s15">
  <span class="num">15</span>
  <h2>Related Standards</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cd span2">
    <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table">
      <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 7</span><span>Business Divestitures — the companion standard governing the DIVESTING entity's side of the same transactions</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 22</span><span>Consolidated Financial Statements — supplies the broader consolidation framework goodwill sits within</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="section-header" id="s16">
  <span class="num">16</span>
  <h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2>
  <span class="line"></span>
</div>
<div class="grid">
  <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item">
      <div class="glossary-term">Partial goodwill method</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">Measuring non-controlling interests at their proportionate share of identifiable net assets, recognising goodwill only for the parent's own acquired interest — J-GAAP's only available approach.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
      <div class="glossary-term">Bargain purchase gain</div>
      <div class="glossary-def">The gain recognised immediately when the fair value of net identifiable assets acquired exceeds the consideration transferred.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="legend">
  <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
  <div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span>Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)<span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span>Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)<span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span>純資産の部<span class="badge be">SCNA</span>Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)<span class="badge bc">SCF</span>Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div>
</div>
<div class="footer-note">ASBJ Statement No. 21 — Accounting Standard for Business Combinations · Extended Reference Poster · Self-contained, no internet connection required</div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
